{
 "thread": "IZlM4CZliz4",
 "subject": "Non-independence of animal observations in open populations",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2019-10-22T21:44:05Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "1wl5QH0sBQAJ",
   "from": "Pat Taggart",
   "author": "b07af4174ebf173b",
   "date": "2019-10-21T22:41:51Z",
   "subject": "Non-independence of animal observations in open populations",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"font-size:small\">Hi All,</span><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">Thanks in advance for any and all help.</div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">I am interested to hear if it is possible to account for non-independence of animal detections in an open populations? and if so how you might go about this?</div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">I have 22 sites. My primary sampling periods are made up of 29 year x season combinations, 2011-2018 with 4 seasons each year except 2018 which only has 1 season. Within each primary sampling period I have 3 replicate rabbit counts. However, as rabbits live in groups in warrens the detection of one rabbit makes it more likely that additional rabbits are also detected - my data violate the assumption of &#39;independence of detection&#39;.</div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">I am keen to estimate rabbit abundance at each of my 22 sites for each year*season combination. This can be done using an open population n-mixture model using the function pcountOpen(), although I am unsure if or how I could account for the non-independence of animal detections within pcountOpen()? Alternatively, is there another way that I could estimate rabbit abundance for each year*season combination at each site whilst also accounting for non-independence of detection? Would my model be fundamentally incorrect to ignore this model assumption in this instance if it is not possible to account for non-independence of detection?</div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">I notice that it is possible to account for non-independence of detection in closed population n-mixture models using a beta-binomial distribution to describe the observation process (Martin et al 2011; Dorazio et al 2013), but I can&#39;t seem to work out how this might or if it can be implemented in unmarked in r.</div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">Thanks again,</div><div style=\"font-size:small\">Pat    </div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "eYNBIsdhBQAJ",
   "from": "John Clare",
   "author": "4a635fe980c8c1cd",
   "date": "2019-10-22T14:58:23Z",
   "subject": "Re: Non-independence of animal observations in open populations",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Pat,<div><br></div><div>I think it is certainly possible to plug the beta-binomial observation model into an open Dail-Madsen type of model. I don&#39;t believe this is something that could be directly implemented using unmarked, but you could pull code from Dorazio et al. or Martin et al. and plug it into code for a standard open model. Maybe using BUGS (and borrowing from Martin et al.) is more intuitive than trying to hard-code the likelihood to be optimized.  Something like the below (you&#39;d need to define priors and the process model, etc.)--hope it helps.</div><div><br></div><div>John</div><div><br></div><div>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">for\r\n(i in 1:R) { # Loops over R sites<u></u><u></u></span></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">   N[i, 1] ~ dpois(lambda[i])  #Initial Abundance at each site </span></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">}<u></u><u></u></span></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><u></u><br><u></u></span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">for (i in 1:R) { # Loops over R sites<u></u><u></u></span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">  for (t in 2:T) { #Loops over the subsequent time periods</span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">   N[i, T] &lt;-S[i, t]+R[i,t]  #survivors + recruits </span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">   S[i, t]~dbinom(phi, N[i, t-1])</span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">   R[i, t]~dpois(gamma*N[s, t-1])</span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">}<u></u><u></u></span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:13.3333px\">}</span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><font face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:13.3333px\"><br></span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">for (i in 1:R) { # Loops over R sites<u></u><u></u></span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">  for (t in 1:T) { #Loops over all time periods</span></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><u></u>   <u></u></span></font><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\">for (k in 1:K) { # Loops over repeated visits or whatever</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">#\r\nObservation model for replicated counts</span></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">C[i, t, k]\r\n~ dbin(p[i, t, k], N[i, t])<u></u><u></u></span></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">        p[i, t, k]~dbeta(alpha.p,beta.p) ##this is the beta-binomial part<u></u><u></u></span></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"> }<u></u><u></u></span></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><u></u> }<u></u></span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><u></u>}<u></u></span></font></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><u></u> <u></u></span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0in;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;text-autospace:none\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Courier New\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"><u></u> <u></u></span></font></p></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
